WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.100
. Let's go next to.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:11.080
Why don't we go to Thibeault from Brussels,
Belgium, Thibeault, you're on the air.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.080
Hi, David.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.080
Can you hear me?

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.390
I can hear you perfectly.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:23.200
So first, I wanted to ask, how are people
coping in America with the stimulus checks?

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:30.281
Because I am raised by a single mother and
she has a beauty salon, and in Belgium, all

00:00:30.281 --> 00:00:35.620
the contact jobs or close contact jobs had
to close for several months.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.600
And my bride is gone every month.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.960
She got an amount of money proportionate to
her income.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:51.520
And I just can't imagine how Americans can
live without paychecks for so many months.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.620
It's it's there's a there's a range.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:59.829
I mean, you know, fortunately lots of people
had jobs that translated to work from home,

00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:01.219
but many people did not.

00:01:01.219 --> 00:01:06.390
Of course, a lot of people are on unemployment
and struggling on unemployment.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:10.170
In fact, we did a story earlier this week
on the bonus show.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.799
That's the award winning bonus show.

00:01:11.799 --> 00:01:17.810
Thibeault, by the way, we did a story on the
bonus show about how one third of those receiving

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:23.229
unemployment in February were struggling to
pay for food and housing so that even people

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:24.900
on unemployment are struggling.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.080
Right now, a lot of younger people have moved
back in with parents.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.409
I mean, there's not one.

00:01:29.409 --> 00:01:30.450
It's a very good question.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.070
There's not one answer.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.600
But different people are coping in different
ways and to different degrees.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:41.280
But it's pathetic what what hasn't been done
here compared to what Europe has done.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:48.280
Yeah, and like I said, also in America, that
also certain jobs had to just close for several

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.729
months, or is it just the state by state legislated?

00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:56.710
Well, the answer is yes, but it was based
on a state by state basis.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:01.380
So there were long periods where in certain
states things like, you know, massage therapy

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:07.490
was just closed, hair salons closed, things
like this, it is decided upon at the state

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:08.490
level.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.799
So there's kind of like a different answers
in different states.

00:02:10.799 --> 00:02:12.559
But, yeah, that was that was the case.

00:02:12.559 --> 00:02:14.439
OK, I understand.

00:02:14.439 --> 00:02:18.549
And I just wanted to ask you also about Bernie
Sanders.

00:02:18.549 --> 00:02:19.549
Sure.

00:02:19.549 --> 00:02:24.930
Like he is, I believe, the chairman of the
Senate Budget Committee.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:25.930
Right.

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:31.859
Like, what is what are his powers or what
can he basically get done for you guys?

00:02:31.859 --> 00:02:32.859
It's not clear.

00:02:32.859 --> 00:02:35.230
I mean, listen, the powers are enumerated.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:37.480
You could just Google exactly what the powers
are.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.579
And it has to do with agenda setting in that
in that committee.

00:02:41.579 --> 00:02:45.209
What he can do unilaterally is very much unclear.

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:49.069
And he's still going to need to build consensus
with the broader Democratic Party, I think.

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:51.449
But it's still a good thing to have him in
that role.

00:02:51.449 --> 00:02:56.799
But if people are expecting Bernie to unilaterally
get a ton done because he's in that position,

00:02:56.799 --> 00:03:01.709
I think that that expectation probably will
leave you disappointed.

00:03:01.709 --> 00:03:07.940
And did the establishment give him that position
or did he get suited to that chair position?

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:09.199
He was voted to.

00:03:09.199 --> 00:03:11.529
He was selected by the Senate for that.

00:03:11.529 --> 00:03:16.859
I mean, isn't that, like, pretty great for
progressive?

00:03:16.859 --> 00:03:17.859
Absolutely.

00:03:17.859 --> 00:03:22.949
I mean, the question will be things are abnormal
right now because of the pandemic.

00:03:22.949 --> 00:03:29.639
Once things normalize, then we will get to
see, OK, from the progressive agenda, what

00:03:29.639 --> 00:03:34.389
will be done and what can Bernie help push
along in his role as Senate budget chairman?

00:03:34.389 --> 00:03:35.699
We just don't know yet.

00:03:35.699 --> 00:03:37.159
Mm hmm.

00:03:37.159 --> 00:03:42.989
Because like in the House, like the progressive
candidates like ABC, yeah, they just didn't

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:44.959
get like any high positions.

00:03:44.959 --> 00:03:45.959
Right.

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:48.219
That is essentially correct.

00:03:48.219 --> 00:03:51.779
And we will see if that changes in 20, 22.

00:03:51.779 --> 00:03:54.540
Yeah, OK, thanks, things and Christians.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.540
Thank you.

